WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.362
This query first trims the email and converts it to lowercase.

00:00:03.362 --> 00:00:07.262
SPLIT_PART then returns the text before or after the at sign.

00:00:07.262 --> 00:00:10.012
POSITION reports where the delimiter occurs.

00:00:10.012 --> 00:00:13.637
Run the query and compare the parsed user and domain values.

00:00:13.637 --> 00:00:17.252
Also notice what happens for missing or malformed email values.

00:00:17.252 --> 00:00:21.442
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:21.442 --> 00:00:27.027
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:29.207
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:29.207 --> 00:00:34.217
In a real setting such as a CRM standardizing names, emails, and phone numbers,

00:00:34.217 --> 00:00:39.882
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:39.882 --> 00:00:41.277
before discussing syntax.

00:00:41.277 --> 00:00:46.880
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
